the closure types once more, and cl names them differently again from both clang and cxx1: it writes a content hash, ??R<lambda_0bdb91391ff8fa3d4622e85ef8ea9375>@@QEBA@XZ, where cxx1 writes ??R$_0@?1??viaThisCapture@S@@QEBAHXZ@QEBAHXZ - and it emits a constructor for each closure where cxx1 builds the object inline and emits none. [expr.prim.lambda] calls a closure a unique unnamed type and obliges nobody to spell it any particular way; nothing links against these, a call operator being reached through its object and the object never leaving the function that made it. lookup-order.nonames records the same difference against clang on all three targets. Every other name in this case agrees with cl, and what the case is about - which of the three scopes owns an unqualified name, [basic.lookup.unqual]/1 - is checked by run-cases.cmd on the box and run.sh on the other two.
